---
title: Building dashboards
summary: A recipe for dashboards with a static shell, tiles as islands, one shared filter object and server data that stays in sync, without a store library.
related:
  - data-state/state-context
  - data-state/remote-functions
  - regions/held-regions
  - app/spa-router
---

Dashboards are the worst case for the usual choice between a static page and an SPA. Rendered statically, a dashboard is dead weight, but shipped as an SPA it hydrates a whole application to show a dozen tiles that are mostly read-only. They are also the best case for ogygia, because a dashboard divides _exactly_ along island lines, and the only real work is knowing which primitive owns which job. This page introduces no new API. It is a map of how the existing pieces, each documented on its own page, combine into filters, cross-filtering tiles, live data and drill-down.

## The anatomy

| Dashboard piece                                       | ogygia primitive                                                             |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Shell: nav, headers, layout chrome                    | Plain page markup (zero JS), [prerendered](/docs/app/spa-router)             |
| Interactive tile: chart with tooltips, sortable table | [Client island](/docs/regions/client-islands) (`wake`)                       |
| Read-only tile: personal or per-request HTML          | [Server island](/docs/regions/server-islands) (`render: 'deferred'`)         |
| Heavy static content inside a tile                    | [Lake](/docs/regions/lakes) (`wake: 'none'`)                                 |
| Global filters: date range, tenant, segment           | One [wire class](/docs/data-state/state-context) provided through context    |
| Tile data                                             | [Remote functions](/docs/data-state/remote-functions) called inside the tile |
| Ticking tile: live numbers, feeds                     | `query.live` that yields [held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions)          |
| Tile that the _server_ selects                        | [Held region](/docs/regions/held-regions) from a registry                    |

The sections below build a dashboard from this table, from the top down.

## One filter object, everywhere

Every dashboard has one core gesture: you change the date range, and all the tiles update. Model the filters as one wire class, and put **all** shared UI state on it, including cross-filter selections:

```ts title=filters.svelte.ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';

export class Filters {
  range = $state('30d');
  segment = $state<string | null>(null);   // set by clicking a chart bar

  static wire = import.meta.og.wire({
    encode: (f: Filters) => $state.snapshot({ range: f.range, segment: f.segment }),
    decode: (d) => Object.assign(new Filters(), d),
  });
}

export const filtersCtx = ogygia.createContext<Filters>('filters');
```

Provide it once, above the tiles:

```svelte title=+page.svelte
<!-- this page ships no JS of its own -->
<script>
  import { Provide } from 'ogygia';
  import RangePicker from './RangePicker.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  import Revenue from './Revenue.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  import TopProducts from './TopProducts.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
  import { Filters, filtersCtx } from './filters.svelte.js';

  const filters = new Filters();
</script>

<Provide values={[filtersCtx(filters)]}>
  <RangePicker />
  <div class="grid">
    <Revenue />
    <TopProducts />
  </div>
</Provide>
```

Each tile is a separate bundle, but all of them hold **one live object**. When the picker writes `filters.range`, every tile that read the context sees the change, without a store library, an event bus or props threaded through the tree. A `visible` tile that hydrates later joins the _current_ instance, selections included. [State & context](/docs/data-state/state-context) explains how the wire contract makes this work.

## Tiles fetch their own data

Each tile owns its query. If you derive the call from the shared filters, the tile fetches again by itself whenever the filters change:

```svelte title=Revenue.svelte
<script>
  import { getStats } from './dashboard.remote';
  import { filtersCtx } from './filters.svelte.js';

  const filters = filtersCtx.get();
  const stats = $derived(getStats(filters.range));
</script>

<article class:stale={stats.loading}>
  {#if stats.current}
    <h3>Revenue</h3>
    <strong>{stats.current.revenue}</strong>
  {/if}
</article>

<style>
  .stale { opacity: 0.6; transition: opacity 150ms; }
</style>
```

Several things here come without any work on your part:

- **No refetch wiring.** `$derived` runs the query again when `filters.range` changes, which is plain Svelte rather than a dashboard framework.
- **No hydration flash.** The SSR pass awaited the query on the server, and the result is seeded into the client cache, so the tile hydrates onto correct HTML without fetching again. This holds in production; in dev it degrades to a refetch.
- **No loading-state plumbing.** The query keeps `.current` (the last settled value) while a new call is in flight, so the old number stays on screen and the tile only dims. Skeletons are for the first paint, not for every filter change.

**Cross-filtering is a field write.** The bar chart sets `filters.segment` in its click handler, and `TopProducts` includes `filters.segment` in its query arguments. The two tiles know nothing about each other, because the shared object does the coordinating:

```svelte
<!-- inside BarChart.svelte -->
<rect onclick={() => (filters.segment = bucket.name)} … />
```

**Shared data is deduplicated for you.** Two tiles that call `getStats(filters.range)` with the same arguments share one cache entry and one request, because Kit's query cache is app-wide and all islands share the one client runtime. Put the expensive aggregate in one query and let the KPI tile, the chart and the table all read it, instead of building a distribution layer.

## Tiles that ship zero JS

Not every tile needs hydration. A "recent orders" list that nobody interacts with can be a [server island](/docs/regions/server-islands), which is per-request HTML with no client bundle:

```svelte
<script>
  import RecentOrders from './RecentOrders.svelte' with { render: 'deferred' };
  import AuditLog from './AuditLog.svelte' with { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'visible' };
</script>
```

Each `render: 'deferred'` hole fetches its own HTML on load, and on an SPA navigation [single-flight navigation](/docs/app/spa-router#single-flight) pulls all of them down in one batch request. Tiles below the fold with `render: 'deferred', fetch: 'visible'` cost the server no render until you scroll to them.

For the tiles you _do_ hydrate, two refinements help:

- **Lakes for heavy content inside.** An interactive tile can hold a large server-rendered SVG chart. If you mark the chart `wake: 'none'` inside the island, the controls hydrate while the chart's markup never enters the client bundle, and `render: 'live'` can refresh it in the background. See [Lakes](/docs/regions/lakes).
- **Held regions when the server chooses the tile.** On a dashboard users can configure, the backend knows the layout, so the client needs no widget registry. A remote returns `region(…)` for each slot from a server-side registry, the page renders `<Region of={…}>` for whatever arrives, and the client never imports widgets the user did not place. See [Held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions).

Keep the trade-off in mind: a server island's props are captured when it is signed, and it renders again per _request_, not per filter change. A tile that has to react to filters is a client island with a query (as above), which can still keep its heavy static parts in a lake.

## Live tiles

For data that ticks, such as active users or a status feed, `query.live` pushes updates and the tile subscribes. If you yield a held region on each tick, the _server_ renders the update:

```ts title=dashboard.remote.ts
export const activeUsers = query.live(v.string(), async function* (range) {
  for await (const n of metrics(range)) yield region(LiveStat, { n });
});
```

```svelte
<Region of={activeUsers(filters.range).current} />
```

A raw (HTML-only) held region **morphs** in, so focus, typed text and open popovers stay. An interactive one is **kept alive** and receives the new props in the mounted island, so its local state, such as a hovered point or an expanded row, survives the tick. [Held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions#live-regions) covers this in detail.

## Drill-down and navigation

Dashboards navigate, for example from an overview to orders to an order's detail. With the [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router), those are real routes with URLs, a working back button and `load` functions, rather than modal state.

- **Filters can follow you.** Give the codec an `id`, and the same live `Filters` instance survives navigation for the length of the session, so the detail page opens already set to the range the user chose. Without the `id`, filters reset on each page. Either is one line, so choose the behaviour your users expect. For dashboards people share, mirror the filters into the URL and merge them in `load`. [State & context](/docs/data-state/state-context#continuity-surviving-navigation) explains the `id`.
- **Expensive tiles can carry over.** `keep: 'chart'` moves the live island across a navigation instead of remounting it, so a rendered chart keeps its zoom level and fetched data, and the new page's props are pushed in.
- **Half-filled forms already carry over.** A settings form left mid-edit is restored when you come back, because continuity is on by default.

## What you don't need to build

If you find yourself building one of these by hand, a primitive already covers it:

| If you're writing…                                | Use instead                                                |
| ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| A store library or an event bus between tiles     | One wire class through context                             |
| Refetch-on-filter-change effects                  | `$derived(query(filters.x))`                               |
| A "share this data between tiles" layer           | One query, many readers. The cache dedupes                 |
| Skeleton or spinner state per tile                | `.current` + `.loading` (stale-while-refetch comes free)   |
| WebSocket plumbing plus diff/patch for live tiles | `query.live` that yields held regions (morph / keep-alive) |
| A client-side widget registry for user layouts    | Held regions: the server selects, the client paints        |
| Lazy tile loading                                 | `wake: 'visible'` / `render: 'deferred', fetch: 'visible'` |
| "Keep the chart alive across pages" hacks         | `keep`                                                     |

## Do / don't

- **Do** put every piece of shared UI state (range, segment, selection) on the one wire class, because two sources of truth make tiles drift apart.
- **Do** set tiles to `wake: 'visible'` and below-the-fold server islands to `render: 'deferred', fetch: 'visible'`. Most of a dashboard's cost is below the fold.
- **Do** dim on `.loading` instead of unmounting to a spinner. Keeping the last value visible is what makes filter changes feel immediate.
- **Don't** make a tile a server island because it "feels server-like". If it has to react to filters, it is a client island with a query, since a server island trades reactivity for per-request HTML with no JavaScript.
- **Don't** use `query.live` for data that only changes when the user acts. A plain query that refetches through `$derived` is cheaper and simpler.
- **Don't** judge data flows only in dev, where SSR query seeding degrades to a refetch. Check a production build before you judge any flash. See [Constraints & patterns](/docs/reference/constraints).
